# decision package.json receives precise dependency edits

The npm adapter changes only the matching `dependencies` entry. Existing inline JSON keeps its
surrounding bytes and unrelated dependencies; no whole-document pretty-print is allowed.
Locked wiring uses the canonical Git URL plus the full locked commit, and drift reads
`package.json` even when no package lock exists.
HTTP(S) and SSH Git dependencies use an explicit `git+` transport. In particular, Yarn Berry
receives `git+ssh://...#commit=<sha>` (with SCP-style SSH URLs normalised first); bare `ssh://`
and `https://` selectors are not valid Yarn Git dependencies. A native `git://` URL stays
`git://` rather than becoming the invalid `git+git://` form.
The adapter always writes `LockedDependency.Git` with the full `LockedDependency.Commit`.

# decision npm pull materializes without lifecycle scripts

Pull always invokes the detected manager and materializes the dependency, including when no
native lock exists and when `node_modules` was deleted at an unchanged commit. npm uses
install/update with `--ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund`; Yarn Berry, pnpm, and Bun use their
corresponding script-disabled install/update operations. Remove edits only the owned root entry
and then invokes the manager so native locks and project-local installation converge without
manually deleting packages still required transitively. Bun uses its native `remove` while the
root declaration is still present, restores the original manifest bytes, then applies the owned
byte-preserving edit; this is required because ordinary `bun install` can retain an extraneous
Git package in `node_modules`. Missing tools are errors, never fallback.

# test npm adapter preservation

- Wire and unwire are idempotent and preserve unrelated inline JSON bytes.
- Tampering with the manifest pin is drift even without `package-lock.json`.
- npm Pull creates/updates the lock and local installation while disabling scripts, audit, and funding requests.
- Yarn Berry output carries a `git+` transport for HTTPS, SSH, and SCP-style inputs.
- Inspect is offline and requires the selected manager lock to associate the root package with
  the full locked commit; the mere presence of `node_modules` or `.pnp.cjs` is insufficient.
